[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Real-time Listeners (onSnapshot) and Event Mechanics in Firebase Firestore"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Firestore provides powerful real-time capabilities through the onSnapshot method. Unlike standard one-time fetch requests (getDocs or getDoc), real-time listeners establish an active, persistent connection to the Firestore database. This allows your application to instantly receive updates whenever data changes on the server, whether modified by the current user, a background process, or another client."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"How onSnapshot Works Under the Hood"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"When you attach an onSnapshot listener to a query or document reference, the Firestore SDK initiates a gRPC stream over HTTP/2. The initial payload delivers the current state of the data. Subsequent payloads only transmit delta changes (added, modified, or removed documents). This efficient event mechanism keeps your UI synchronized with minimal network overhead and optimized battery consumption on mobile devices."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Listening to a Single Document"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"To listen to real-time updates on a single document reference, pass a callback function to the onSnapshot method. The callback receives a DocumentSnapshot object containing the latest data."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"import { doc, onSnapshot } from "firebase/firestore";

const unsub = onSnapshot(doc(db, "cities", "SF"), (doc) => {
  console.log("Current data: ", doc.data());
});"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Listening to Multiple Documents (Queries)"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can also apply listeners to complex queries. The resulting QuerySnapshot object exposes a documentChanges() method, which provides granular insight into what specifically changed since the last snapshot event."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"import { collection, query, where, onSnapshot } from "firebase/firestore";

const q = query(collection(db, "cities"), where("state", "==", "CA"));
const unsubscribe = onSnapshot(q, (querySnapshot) => {
  querySnapshot.docChanges().forEach((change) => {
    if (change.type === "added") {
      console.log("New city: ", change.doc.data());
    }
    if (change.type === "modified") {
      console.log("Modified city: ", change.doc.data());
    }
    if (change.type === "removed") {
      console.log("Removed city: ", change.doc.data());
    }
  });
});"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Document Change Events"},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"added: Triggered when a new document matches the query criteria for the first time."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"modified: Triggered when the contents of a document that already matches the query change."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"removed: Triggered when a document has been deleted or modified such that it no longer matches the query."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Managing Memory and Unsubscribing"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Real-time listeners remain active indefinitely. If you leave a listener attached when a component unmounts or a view changes, it will continue to consume network bandwidth and device memory, potentially causing memory leaks or exceeding your Firestore read quotas. Always call the unsubscribe function returned by onSnapshot when the listener is no longer needed."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"// Call the returned function to detach the listener
unsubscribe();"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Handling Errors Gracefully"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"Network issues, security rule violations, or invalid queries can cause listener streams to fail. You should always supply an error callback as the second argument to onSnapshot to handle these exceptions gracefully."},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"const unsub = onSnapshot(doc(db, "cities", "SF"), 
  (doc) => {
    console.log("Data: ", doc.data());
  },
  (error) => {
    console.error("Error fetching real-time update: ", error);
  }
);"}]
